    <meta name="dc.description" content="This study explores the influence of ultrasonic&amp;nbsp;integrated&amp;nbsp;hot air drying technology on the drying characteristics and quality of hawthorn. The results show that at 70&#8451;, ultrasonic integrated&amp;nbsp;hot air drying makes hawthorn slices reach the drying endpoint in 140&amp;nbsp;min, shortening time by 39.1% vs hot air drying. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) observations revealed that higher ultrasonic power lead to an increase in the number and diameter of fine pores on the hawthorn surface. Higher ultrasonic power increases surface pores; this drying has better rehydration ratio and alleviates browning. Total organic acid first rises then falls in it but decreases continuously in hot air drying. LF-NMR shows ultrasonic power shortens moisture signal disappearance time. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) showed that at 60&#8451;, the moisture signal hawthorn disappeared after 120&amp;nbsp;min of hot air drying, and the time required for moisture signal disappearance shortened with increasing ultrasonic power. Ultrasonic integrated hot air drying can effectively increase the drying rate, shorten the drying time, and improve the quality of hawthorn. The cavitation and mechanical effects of ultrasound increase the number and size of the fine pores in the hawthorn slices, loosen the surface tissue structure, and thereby facilitate moisture migration and diffusion during the drying process. The ultrasonic&amp;nbsp;integrated&amp;nbsp;hot air drying technology can significantly enhance the drying efficiency and improve the quality of products."/>
